EDITORS' CHOICE: HIGHLIGHTS OF THE RECENT LITERATURE


EVOLUTION: Under Strict Control

Stephen J. Simpson



In many species of animals, males and females are strikingly (and visibly)=20
different. Three evolutionary genetic pathways can produce such sexual=20
dimorphism. From the outset, the expression of the alleles for dimorphic=20
traits could be limited to one sex. Or the alleles could be expressed=20
initially in both sexes but subsequently promoted only in one sex or=20
repressed only in the other. Darwin believed that the bright colors and=20
ornaments of males were male-limited from their first appearance, whereas=20
Fisher held that they had been lost from females through the accumulation=20
of suppressors. Coyne et al. weigh these alternatives by examining
female hybrids between bird species [such as Costa's (left) and=20
black-chinned (right) hummingbirds] whose males show distinct ornamental=20
traits (for example, plumage patterns and the presence or absence of=20
elongated feathers). Unless the suppressors are completely dominant, these=20
females should show some expression of the male traits. In none of the 13=20
hybrid cases (from four orders and eight families) that met their search=20
criteria did the females express a male-limited trait. This suggests that=20
male ornaments were sex-limited at their first appearance (perhaps by=20
hormonal or physiological differences between the sexes) or are now=20
controlled by completely dominant modifier alleles or regulatory elements.=20
-- SJS
Evolution 62, 214 (2008).




PLANETARY SCIENCE: Telling Time on Mars

Brooks Hanson



Our primary information on the surface age and geologic history of Mars=20

comes from crater densities on different parts of the planet and from=20

dating of Martian meteorites (though their source region on Mars is not=20

known with certainty). Crater ages imply that most of the crust is over 2=20

billion years old. In contrast, dating of one distinct group of Martian=20

meteorites, by a variety of isotopic systems, has generally indicated=20

young ages, from a few hundred thousand to about 1 billion years. These=20

numbers imply that Mars had enough recent magmatic activity to be sampled=20

by a significant fraction of the available Martian meteorites. A problem,=20

however, is that all of these rocks show evidence of alteration and impact

events (required for but not limited to their excavation from Mars), as=20

well as hints of the very early geochemical history of Mars, so=20

interpreting their ages is not generally straightforward. Bouvier et=20

al. present additional analyses of two of the purportedly young=20

meteorites using several isotopic systems and evaluate the ages of all of=20

the Martian meteorites. In general, the Sm-Nd and Lu-Hf ages derived from=20

minerals were young, but the Pb-Pb whole-rock ages were much older (~4=20

billion years). In contrast to some other recent analyses, the authors=20

interpret this difference, and ultimately the other young ages, as=20

indicating later alteration that has re-initialized mineral-based=20

isochrons. -- BH
Earth Planet. Sci. Lett.
266, 105 (2008).




APPLIED PHYSICS: Plasmons on a Wire

Ian S. Osborne



The miniaturization of optical elements such as waveguides is limited by=20

the wavelength of light, typically from a few hundred nanometers up to the

micrometer scale. By comparison, electrical circuits can be patterned down

to a few tens of nanometers. A promising route for integrating these two=20

scales is direct conversion of an optical signal into an electrical one=20

via surface plasmons created at the surface of metal dielectric=20

interfaces. Schmidt et al. use a photonic crystal fiber as the=20

optical carrier and show that they can fill in the air holes that run the=20

length of the fiber with gold or silver, introduced in molten form, to=20

create an array of metal nanowires embedded within the silica matrix.=20

Characterization of the hybrid structure shows that plasmon resonances are

excited in the nanowires, thereby demonstrating the direct integration of=20

optics and nanoelectronics. -- ISO
Phys. Rev.=20

B 77, 33417 (2008).




PLANT SCIENCE: Resisting Resistance

Laura M. Zahn

Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) toxins are=20
bacterial proteins that kill some insects and are widely used in crops for=20
pest control. Plants expressing the Bt toxins Cry1Ac and Cry1Ab=20
make up the majority of insect-resistant transgenic corn and cotton,=20
respectively. A major concern has been that the pests will develop=20
resistance; refuges, where non-transgenic plants grow next to transgenic=20
plants, have been hypothesized to delay the evolution of resistance because
resistant individuals will mate with susceptible individuals from the=20
refuge and hence not confer resistance (if inheritance is recessive) to=20
their offspring.
Tabashnik et al. have analyzed field studies from around the world
to determine the effectiveness of the refuge strategy. They found that=20
resistance has evolved in populations of the corn earworm at some U.S.=20
locations, but not elsewhere nor in five other pests. Furthermore they=20
conclude that the refuge strategy has been successful in slowing the=20
evolution of resistance and that expressing multiple Bt toxins in=20
single plants may further slow the appearance of resistance. --=20
LMZ
Nat. Biotechnol. 26, 199 (2008).




CHEMISTRY: Air to the Rescue

Jake Yeston



Chemists generally take pains to ensure the purity of their reagents, lest=20
a highly reactive contaminant doom the anticipated outcome. Once in a=20
while, though, an adventitious impurity proves more help than hindrance.=20
The reported synthesis of quinine by Woodward and Doering in 1944 was a=20
landmark achievement in the history of organic chemistry, but in the=20
ensuing years, controversy swelled over the report's reliance on a final=20
reduction step published 26 years earlier by Rabe and Kindler. Was the=20
chemistry in this last step really viable? Had Woodward taken his synthesis
all the way to fruition, or actually stopped at the penultimate step? In a=20
meticulous effort to reproduce the Rabe study, Smith and Williams now find=20
that modern concerns probably arose on account of excessively pure=20
materials. They show that the reduction proceeds most efficiently when the=20
aluminum powder called for in the published procedure has been partially=20
oxidized by exposure to air, a reasonably likely circumstance in 1918. --=20
JSY
Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 47, 1736 (2008).




IMMUNOLOGY: Exceptional Acceptance

Stephen J. Simpson



In 1960, Medawar and Burnet were awarded the Nobel Prize for Physiology or=20
Medicine for showing that immunological tolerance to foreign grafts can be=20
acquired. What this and many studies in animal models have shown since is=20
that it is possible to "re-educate" the immune system of the=20
recipient to the foreign antigens carried by the donor cells and grafts.=20
The induction of full immune tolerance to transplanted organs has also been
pursued in the clinic because this would dispense with the need for=20
extensive immunosuppressive regimes currently used to prevent organ=20
rejection.
Scandling et al. and Kawai et al. report cases of human=20
kidney transplantation in which hematopoietic cells from the donor were=20
co-transplanted with the organ, followed by distinct protocols of=20
conditioning involving depletion of host cells. In the first study, this=20
resulted in ongoing mixed chimerism; meaning that there were comparable=20
proportions of donor and recipient hematopoietic cells circulating in the=20
body. In the second study, the extent of chimerism was considerably less=20
and was also transient. Nevertheless, in both clinical settings it was=20
possible to discontinue immunosuppressive drugs without significant adverse
responses to the grafted organs; furthermore, cellular evidence of=20
immunological tolerance was detected. In a third study, Alexander et=20
al. describe a female liver transplant patient in which a spontaneous=20
switch occurred in the blood group and--because the donor was male--the sex
of the circulating leukocytes. Impressively, the removal of=20
immunosuppressive drugs, which was undertaken to resolve hemolytic anemia=20
in the patient, resulted in complete chimerism (meaning that all=20
hematopoietic cells were of donor origin) and long-term acceptance of the=20
grafted liver. These three studies reveal that immunological acceptance of=20
transplanted organs is a realistic goal worthy of further exploration. --=20
SJS
N. Engl. J. Med. 358, 362; 353; 369 (2008).




PHYSIOLOGY: We're Never Alone

Katrina L. Kelner



Each of us carries a community of microorganisms in our intestinal tract=20
that is necessary for optimal health but varies in composition from person=20
to person. To learn which gut microbes most influence human physiology, Li=20
et al. surveyed these symbionts in seven members of a=20
four-generation Chinese family. By sequencing rRNA genes, they assembled a=20
phylogenetic picture of the resident microbes-mainly Bacteroidetes
and Firmicutes spp. The Chinese microbiomes showed individual and=20
male/female differences in their composition, although all differed=20
markedly from those seen in Americans. A parallel analysis of urine samples
from the same family allowed the authors to correlate variations in=20
resident microbes with variations in excreted metabolites, which served as=20
a proxy for the individuals' metabolic state. They found several microbial=20
species that appeared to significantly affect their hosts: for instance,=20
Faecalibacterium prausnitzii was associated with the presence of=20
dimethylamine, suggested to be an indicator of metabolic syndrome and=20
diabetes. This functional metagenomics approach can be extended by=20
combining full genomic sequencing of the microbes with measurements of=20
metabolic or clinical features of interest. Ultimately, the hope is to=20
identify which gut inhabitants are associated with disease states and which
with human well-being. -- KK
Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A. 105, 2117 (2008).





